{{also|Bote|boté|bóte|böte|botë}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[bot#Old_English|bot]]
* [[boot#Etymology_2|boot]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|en|bote|bōte|advantage, benefit, profit; relief, salvation; atonement, amends, expiation; cure}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|bōt||help, relief, advantage, remedy; compensation for an injury or wrong; (peace) offering, recompense, amends, atonement, reformation, penance, repentance}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*bōtō||recompense}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/boʊt/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|boten}}

# The [[atonement]], [[compensation]], [[amends]], [[satisfaction]], [[penance]], [[expiation]]; as, [[manbote]], a compensation for a man slain.
#: ''Iesu {{...}} For synne þat hath my soule bounde, Let þi blessed blood be my '''bote'''.'' &mdash; Iesu þat art hevene<!-- Middle English citation-->
# A [[payment]] of any kind.
# A [[privilege]] or [[allowance]] of necessaries, especially in [[feudal]] times.
# {{lb|en|legal|historical}} A right to take wood from property not one's own.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[repair]]s
#: ''Þey shulde..do '''bote''' to brugges þat to-broke were.'' &mdash; Pier's Plowman, 1400<!-- Middle English citation-->
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[advantage]], [[benefit]], [[profit]], [[cure]], [[remedy]]
#: ''Heo lufeden bi wurten, bi moren, and bi rote; nas þer nan oðer '''boten'''.'' &mdash; Layamon's Brut, 1275<!-- Middle English citation-->

====Usage notes====
* Often used to form compounds indicating a right to take wood only for a specific purpose.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|estovers}}

